tƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;USAJBnmo9zsVM5EUYpgFyeoK31ovR-hVJFdYDAjkonMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyThe Institute for Strategic Dialogue, which tracks the far right, found that legacy accelerationist networks â€” Iron March, the Atomwaffen Division, the Terrorgram Collective â€” read these attackers as undisciplined and ideologically confused rather than as their own.
That disavowal matters, because those networks once supplied exactly what these teenagers lacked: doctrine, hierarchy, and gatekeepers who decided who counted.
In their place, ISD analyst Steven Rai documents [a far stranger set of influences](https://www.isdglobal.org/digital-dispatch/san-diego-mosque-shooting-highlights-new-generation-of-neo-nazi-accelerationism/) â€” True Crime Community fandoms that treat mass killers as celebrities, incel forums, and parasocial attachments to fictional characters from a Dutch cartoon and a video game, sitting alongside the "Great Replacement" and the white-supremacist "saints culture" that venerates past shooters.
The FBI says the two met online.
What held their worldview together was not a manifesto but a feed.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;mLfb9S-k8Wh1RhnBfk4Nd_Gx0wIB2C3IlwOTCUhJ1pEƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hNPZsAeq9RFiZYXpe3VPKGERGN4TmdW02OO8ZYAywnkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkb86ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;GTNSFaCYYEcZKuCRZUZVI7jNlvwLgcZmuymphn-c20wƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Y4PiegydxcROuOSl8MeUPozIstfxBNGSxha98zKRpO0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;kZ7L8GD9uPPJ_4-BA4Fl3EkUG1xqHq6pd6g6p4yQhJwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky
The most telling reading came from somewhere the far right would never look.
Analyzing Islamic State-linked dark-web communities, GNET researcher Muskan Sangwan found jihadist supporters dissecting the San Diego attackers through [internet culture rather than theology](https://gnet-research.org/2026/06/15/from-reaction-to-retaliation-how-islamic-state-dark-web-ecosystems-processed-the-san-diego-mosque-shooting/) â€” their aesthetics, online personas, and meme references.
One supporter named the result "TikTok ideology": a worldview shaped by algorithmically recommended content rather than coherent belief.
