MCFGkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bURs_MmXvXCmOXOSPCR5rS02G_RCEQGEbco34jUlihwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgchangesƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bVjMemUJS00foLLU2rev2LfEt3Mbb6Hv9ZLEGLuiuHcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyhProof-of-Liquidity is designed to address some of the inefficiencies of traditional consensus models, particularly in decentralized finance (DeFi).
Unlike PoW, which requires massive computational power, or PoS, which relies on token holdings, PoL leverages the availability and movement of assets within a network.
This means that users who actively provide liquidityâ€”such as through decentralized exchanges (DEXs) or lending protocolsâ€”play a crucial role in maintaining the blockchainâ€™s security and functionality.

