Java's survival past SunWorld did not end its troubles. In 1996, Microsoft licensed Java for Windows, then added proprietary APIs and declined to support others â€” a product known as Microsoft's J++. Sun alleged that the changes were designed to undermine Java's cross-platform compatibility and steer developers toward Windows-specific implementations. The legal dispute consumed years of engineering resources. "I spent days in deposition talking about this under oath," Lindholm recalled. The disputes concluded with Microsoft paying Sun nearly $2 billion through a series of settlements, per The Register.

The documentary also covers the informal open-source program Lindholm ran before that term entered common use. Sun distributed the Java binary runtime as a free download and provided source code to anyone who requested it; thousands did. The Java internet domain attracted so much traffic that the team ran what The Register describes as a pirate T3 line into the office. Sun eventually invited Richard Stallman to discuss formal open-source release, but he struck Sun executives as "too radical," Lindholm recalled. Sun would not officially release Java as open source for another decade.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EjBilD8TsadaupxuNR3fQeTYvvs0vOEbhR5eX8Xris0ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;p7Tg8__Sm5fSgvvBZbs3CwmCq5ncq25o-tEOatI_Fnoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkb53ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bETR63RGDiuas-O0Jftr6u5lYRK9cPk4YlPwSzQPlgQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;eU0osMLqN3L9HN6prLACnkZhpaBrZXHXjRGziPv9hI8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jHJLntlaGWOoMIs9M9FvdzLAuhUx2TqPxHnlzXUwxpsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyg## The film and the company behind it

*The Java Story* joins a genre that Cult.Repo â€” short for Culture Repository â€” helped establish: professionally produced documentaries on programming languages, with earlier entries covering C++, Python, and React. Those films originated with Honeypot, a tech recruiting platform that funded them to build its developer audience. In 2019, Honeypot was acquired by XING, which later rebranded as New Work SE. Cult.Repo founder Emma Tracey subsequently bought the production company back from New Work SE, reuniting the original team, according to The Register. *The Java Story* is Cult.Repo's first release under independent ownership.

The film features Gosling alongside Mark Reinhold and Brian Goetz, both senior Oracle Java architects. Where earlier entries in the series required, as The Register put it, "a Hollywood-style effort to construct a Hero's Journey" around slower progressions, Java arrived with what the same publication calls "a veritable Love Island of dramas" already in the record â€” and enough of them, it turns out, to fill a documentary without embellishment.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;stw-Z8Go7kkFBsGuaGDv7GL5Fn0jmUl9-gDBx7pBxv4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcurrentƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DxcRs5sUgKUR2GflI4Aom_PZzy8_QNVkDGFI6J3_6Ekƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;F1C4sNtl_RbDRNrYcJU0rt081AflgnxBqREx5GJHFRIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkf288128ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1CEa29yT2PlgWt8DnV1CT8LPi427yrfqLrr8Hy_d8mgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;sf0l197yHija9SuEI99ZWbtEkdIsb92Tb5GIQEradWYƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;dJy27B_xB3Ie03XPbKLq2HztK2Il9-HeSBhLrgiXs74ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;RTl_f0fRzpAqA_3qsZloNZnZMQlOCeyRyB8W09v-r20ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2T2Og3Spitq0vMKJRiVPlJV7XyFe5GoQLuRRVxqqdIAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgchangesƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;E0IcWEQUM_vEvNlq2XSQ-F9mg0onjcUvrolBClfd8Q0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒer